## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses dynamic programming fundamentals through practical string segmentation, testing understanding of both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic Programming: memoization vs. tabulation
- String matching optimization (startsWith, trie)
- Time and space trade-offs between DP approaches
- State definition and transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function minWordSegmentsTopDown(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
  memo[n] = 0;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    let minSegs = Infinity;
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        const result = dfs(i + word.length);
        if (result !== -1) {
          minSegs = Math.min(minSegs, 1 + result);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[i] = minSegs === Infinity ? -1 : minSegs;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulated solution
function minWordSegmentsBottomUp(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[n] = 0;

  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        if (dp[i + word.length] !== Infinity) {
          dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], 1 + dp[i + word.length]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[0];
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time:** O(n × m × L) where n = |s|, m = dict size, L = avg word length. Using a Trie reduces this to O(n × L).
- **Space:** O(n) for the memo/DP array.

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Is Asked

The question tests ability to derive a linear-time, in-place solution for a data integrity problem common in interview tasks; relies on cyclic sort to exploit value-domain mapping, and requires careful handling of duplicates and missing numbers.

## Key Concepts

- In-place cyclic sort
- Handling duplicates and missing using array as hashmap
- Time/space complexity analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
function findMissingAndDuplicate(arr){
  const n = arr.length;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    while(arr[i]>=1 && arr[i]<=n && arr[arr[i]-1] !== arr[i]){
      const target = arr[i]-1;
      [arr[i], arr[target]] = [arr[target], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  let missing = -1, dup = -1;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    if(arr[i] !== i+1){
      missing = i+1;
      dup = arr[i];
      break;
    }
  }
  return {missing, dup};
}
```

